From c7e9e8e18d36ff7523a58641ef375b47681e4410 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: soulexit <metehan.tanriverdi01@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:03:53 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 77/78] panvk/jm: fewer GPU jobs per draw for ARMSX2,
 hardware-side GPU profile aids

Mali hardware counters on a Galaxy A51 (Mali-G72) in ARMSX2 (NFS Most
Wanted) showed fragment work equal to the vendor driver's but 3.2x its
vertex/tiler time per frame, from 3.25 GPU jobs per draw against 1.45.

- Descriptor copies on the CPU (jm-v7 122): the per-draw desc_copy
  compute job is replaced by the same copies done while recording, which
  is valid because a bound descriptor set cannot change unless it is
  UPDATE_AFTER_BIND. The job is kept for such sets, direct texture/sampler
  tables and tables without a CPU mapping. PANVK_DESC_COPY_CPU=0: the job.
- CPU index range for the PS2 emulators (jm-v7 123): the existing
  record-time index scan (PANVK_V7_CPU_INDEX_RANGE) is on by default for
  ARMSX2/AetherSX2/NetherSX2/PCSX2, which write their index stream before
  recording. It removes the per-batch index range helper job, its barrier
  and the GPU patching of every vertex job. A TRANSFER_DST index buffer
  now only counts as GPU-written once a transfer write into it was
  recorded (panvk_buffer::gpu_written).
- GPU profile: the fragment shader hash of each batch (fs=, matches the
  MESA_SPIRV_DUMP_PATH file name), descriptor copy and render pass merge
  candidate counters; PANVK_IMG_LOG=1 logs each image's layout.

NFS Most Wanted on the A51: 13 -> 17-18 fps (vendor driver 21.5), with
PANVK_V7_IDVS=1. CTS on the A51 matches 1.0.002 with the defaults and
with IDVS + CPU index range on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
